# "Allocator traffic" (ranked opportunity #6) measures NULL on this box

date: 2026-08-07
host: Apple M4 Pro (12 logical cores), darwin 25.5.0
config: 8-bit 4:2:0, still/allintra (hierarchical_levels=0, intra_period=1),
        CQP qp40, tiles 0/0, single-threaded, gradient, no target-cpu=native
harness: tools/perf_ab.sh — interleaved paired, randomised per-round order,
         median of paired ratios + p25/p75, neither side niced

## The claim

The 2026-08-07 perf attribution ranked allocator traffic #6: "6.5 % of p6,
7.7 % of p10, 12 % of p2; 98-334x C … Bit-identical-safe, high tractability
(hoist to scratch)". A re-profile taken AFTER the transform port raised the
malloc/free + memset/memmove/bzero family to **15.47 % of preset-6 self time**
at 512x512 — the largest single category on the profile at that point.

It does not convert to wall clock.

## What was tried, and what it measured

**Shipped (kept, because it is a real win):** `tx_unit`'s five purely-internal
buffers moved to a per-thread `TxScratch`, removing 5 of the 7 heap allocations
per transform unit — the highest-frequency allocation site in the encoder.
That measured **1.014x-1.034x** (commit "reuse tx_unit's five internal
buffers"). So the category is not entirely inert; the *first* 5-of-7 was worth
2-3 %.

**Everything after that measured null or negative.** Three further rounds,
each byte-identical (120/120 cells) and each A/B'd on its own:

| change | 128 p2 | 128 p6 | 128 p10 | 512 p2 | 512 p6 | 512 p10 |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| hadamard_satd + coeff_contexts x2 as STACK arrays | 1.000x | **0.983x** | 1.008x | 1.002x | 1.006x | 1.008x |
| the same three as per-thread SCRATCH (n=11) | 0.997x | 0.996x | 1.016x | 0.995x | 1.002x | **0.985x** |
| compute_stats' `d`/`s` planes as per-thread scratch (n=11) | — | 0.984x | 1.022x | — | 1.000x | 1.006x |

Every one of those straddles 1.0. The stack-array form is actively WORSE at
128x128 p6 (0.983x): `[i16; 1024]` + `[i32; 1024]` costs a 6 KB zero-fill per
`hadamard_satd` call, which is more than the small malloc it replaced. The
per-thread-scratch form removes the zero-fill but pays a `RefCell` borrow plus
a `Drop` return, and comes out at 0.985x at 512 p10.

Only the `compute_stats` scratch was kept, and only because the allocation it
removes was introduced by the Wiener rewrite in the same series (~270 KB per
restoration unit) — cleaning up one's own regression, not a measured win. The
`hadamard_satd` / `coeff_contexts` changes were reverted.

## Reading

`sample` attributes 1 ms wall-clock ticks to whatever is on top of the stack.
On macOS a large share of the time that lands in the malloc family is page-fault
and zone-lock work that simply moves elsewhere when the call is removed. A
15 % self-time share in malloc/memset is therefore NOT a 15 % opportunity here.

**Do not spend more time on allocation removal in this encoder without first
demonstrating a wall-clock delta on a small pilot.** The remaining known sites
are `TxUnitOut::{qcoeff, recon}` (2 allocations per transform unit, moved out
of the struct by callers so a recycling pool would need a `PooledVec` newtype
and a 5-site refactor) and `pd0`'s returned `qcoeff`/`dqcoeff`. Given the
numbers above, neither is likely to pay.

---

# Addendum: hoisting compute_stats' row loop into a 2D dot is a REGRESSION

date: 2026-08-07 (same session)

After the Wiener rewrite, `restoration::compute_stats` was still the biggest
named function on the preset-6 profile at 9.66 % of self time. The obvious next
step looked like eliminating the per-row work: the NEON path does one dot
product per (k, t) pair PER REGION ROW, so it pays a horizontal reduction plus
an i64 read-modify-write 1225 * height times at win=7. Accumulating across many
rows in the vector registers and draining once per block should remove almost
all of that — and the i32 lanes have room for it (a 256x256 unit fits in a
single block: 33025 / ceil(width/4) rows).

Implemented as `dot2d_i16_neon(a, a_stride, b, b_stride, rows, width)` with the
drain cadence derived from the overflow bound. Byte-identical (120/120 cells,
c_parity_wiener 5/5 green).

MEASURED — tools/perf_ab.sh, interleaved paired, randomised order, n=13,
neither side niced:

| cell | before | after | speedup |
|---|---|---|---|
| 128x128 p6 |  2.519 ms |  2.589 ms | **0.977x** |
| 256x256 p6 | 13.505 ms | 13.984 ms | **0.964x** |
| 512x512 p6 | 42.081 ms | 44.742 ms | **0.944x** |

2-6 % SLOWER, consistently, with p25/p75 entirely above 1.0. Reverted.

The horizontal reduce was not the bottleneck; adding the row loop inside the
vector function (row slicing, re-created `chunks_exact` iterators per row, and
more live state across the loop) costs more than the reduces it saves. The
`kernel_tiers` microbench could not settle this — its scalar arm swung 590 ->
558 -> 330 us across three runs on a box shared with another agent, a 1.8x
range with no code change. **Use the encoder-level interleaved paired A/B for
decisions here; treat the microbench as a sanity check only.**

Patch preserved at `~/tmp/dspgap/dot2d_regression.patch` for the session; the
next attempt on compute_stats should go after the ALGORITHM (C's delta /
`derive_triangle_win7_neon` incremental update, which exploits the sliding
window's overlap) rather than the loop shape.
